WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.090
<v Examiner>Exam case 3, continued.

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.989
<v Examiner>The four lung micrographs.

3
00:00:04.989 --> 00:00:05.719
<v Examiner>Ten marks.

4
00:00:06.169 --> 00:00:10.136
<v Examiner>Her four lung micrographs, A to D, from the case study workbook.

5
00:00:10.136 --> 00:00:12.802
<v Examiner>Name the part of the airway each one shows.

6
00:00:12.802 --> 00:00:17.327
<v Examiner>Give its lining, and say whether cartilage and smooth muscle are present.

7
00:00:17.777 --> 00:00:19.339
This table isn't starred.

8
00:00:19.339 --> 00:00:24.774
But her exam case study helpline links a deck called Case Study Lung Histology for Exam

9
00:00:24.774 --> 00:00:29.335
under this case, and she says the booklet's diagrams will be in the exam.

10
00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:30.459
So learn all four.

11
00:00:30.459 --> 00:00:35.270
The wording of the question and the letters here follow the workbook's order.

12
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.419
The hook: cartilage stops at the bronchiole.

13
00:00:38.419 --> 00:00:40.136
Muscle stops at the alveoli.

14
00:00:40.586 --> 00:00:42.150
Letter A, the trachea.

15
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.559
It's lined with ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium.

16
00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:47.910
Cartilage: present.

17
00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.546
Smooth muscle: present.

18
00:00:49.996 --> 00:00:51.568
Letter B, a bronchus.

19
00:00:51.568 --> 00:00:55.385
Also ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium.

20
00:00:55.385 --> 00:00:57.107
Smooth muscle: present.

21
00:00:57.107 --> 00:00:58.529
Cartilage: present.

22
00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:01.613
Letter C, a bronchiole.

23
00:01:01.613 --> 00:01:03.815
Lined with cuboidal epithelium.

24
00:01:03.815 --> 00:01:05.094
Cartilage: absent.

25
00:01:05.094 --> 00:01:06.728
Smooth muscle: present.

26
00:01:06.728 --> 00:01:12.908
Smooth muscle with no cartilage to hold the tube open is what lets a bronchiole squeeze

27
00:01:12.908 --> 00:01:13.974
down in asthma.

28
00:01:14.424 --> 00:01:15.892
Letter D, the alveoli.

29
00:01:15.892 --> 00:01:21.430
Mostly type one pneumocytes, which are squamous epithelium: thin, for gas exchange.

30
00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.700
A smaller number of type two pneumocytes, which make surfactant.

31
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.769
And macrophages, which keep the alveoli clean.

32
00:01:28.769 --> 00:01:30.637
No cartilage, and no muscle.

33
00:01:32.087 --> 00:01:34.735
And her last line ties it back to Ane.

34
00:01:34.735 --> 00:01:39.055
The smooth muscle is relaxed by bronchodilators: the reliever.

35
00:01:39.055 --> 00:01:44.141
Cortisol, taken daily, is the preventer, and it reduces the inflammation.

36
00:01:44.591 --> 00:01:45.169
<v Examiner>Once more.

37
00:01:45.169 --> 00:01:46.383
<v Examiner>The four micrographs.

38
00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:53.390
The trachea and a bronchus: ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium,

39
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.422
with cartilage and smooth muscle.

40
00:01:55.422 --> 00:01:59.794
A bronchiole: cuboidal epithelium, with smooth muscle but no cartilage.

41
00:01:59.794 --> 00:02:04.228
The alveoli: type one pneumocytes, squamous epithelium for gas exchange;

42
00:02:04.228 --> 00:02:08.416
type two pneumocytes for surfactant; macrophages to keep them clean;

43
00:02:08.416 --> 00:02:10.078
and no cartilage or muscle.

44
00:02:10.078 --> 00:02:14.450
Bronchodilators relax the smooth muscle; daily cortisol, the preventer,

45
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.990
reduces the inflammation.
